---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../../components/Nav.astro';
import '../../../styles/global.css';
---
<Base
  title="History — Prologue"
  description="From the legend of Solomon's gold to the VOC mine shaft blueprints — the prologue to the history of Nagari Salido, Minangkabau Coast."
  image="/img/RP-P-1896-A-19368-994.jpg"
>
  <Nav lang="en" />

  <main class="prolog-page">

    <header class="prolog-header">
      <p class="prolog-chapter">Chapter 1</p>
      <h1 class="prolog-title">Prologue</h1>
    </header>

    <article class="prolog-body">

      <p>Since time immemorial, gold — that most coveted of metals — has been hunted by humankind.
      To pursue it is to drink salt water: there is no satisfaction until one reaches the source.</p>

      <p>Some local communities imagined that source as a mare — a mare that bolts whenever pursued.
      Running. Vanishing into the forest, with no trace of where it has gone.</p>

      <p>The hunt, then, has never been straightforward. It is difficult — filled with tragedy.
      And so it is, more often than not, wrapped in mythology.</p>

      <p>The world's center of gold, so the story goes, lies in Sumatra.</p>

      <p>The Old Testament, in the Book of Kings (10:11), records:</p>

      <blockquote>
        "Moreover, the fleet of Hiram, which carried gold from Ophir, brought from Ophir
        a very great amount of almug wood and precious stones."
      </blockquote>

      <p>That Ophir, so the legend goes, was in Sumatra. And so one legend piles upon the next.</p>

      <p>This image of a legendary source of gold echoes throughout the epic poem
      <em>Os Lusíadas</em> by Luís Vaz de Camões:</p>

      <blockquote lang="pt">
        "Dizem que desta terra co as possantes ondas o mar, entrando, dividiu
        a nobre ilha Samatra, que já d'antes juntas ambas a gente antiga viu.
        Quersoneso foi dita; e das prestantes veias d'ouro que a terra produziu,
        'Aurea', por epitéto lhe ajuntaram; alguns que fosse Ofir imaginaram."
        <cite>— Luís Vaz de Camões, <em>Os Lusíadas</em>, 1572</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>In translation, roughly: <em>It is said that from this mainland, with powerful waves,
      the sea entered and divided the noble island of Sumatra. It was called the Peninsula;
      and for the precious veins of gold that its earth produced, the epithet "Aurea" —
      Golden Land — was bestowed; some imagined this to be Ophir.</em></p>

      <p>A similar picture appears in the <em>Relação da Viagem e Naufrágio da Nao S. Paulo</em>,
      a Portuguese mariner's account from five centuries ago:</p>

      <blockquote>
        "...there is found within it [Sumatra] every wealth that a mortal soul could desire,
        great stores of very fine gold from Monancabo, which every year is carried to Malacca
        in the amount of twelve to fifteen quintals; and it is from here that it is believed to be
        <strong>the gold sought by Solomon, and his ships carried that gold for the
        construction of the Holy Temple</strong>."
      </blockquote>

      <p>From these ancient accounts, Sumatra emerges as one of the world's most significant
      centers of gold. It is no surprise that the name Swarnadwipa became attached to it —
      the Island of Gold.</p>

      <p>Beyond Ophir as Sumatra's foremost gold center, Salido is mentioned as another such
      point — ancient, and perhaps significant. In the Sultan of Aceh's letter to King James I
      of England in the seventeenth century, the name appears:</p>

      <blockquote>
        "...raja yang mengampukan perbendahara'an deri-pada seni 'mas, dan seni perak,
        dan deri-pada galian 'mas yang dalam...pada gunong negri Salida."
      </blockquote>

      <hr class="prolog-divider" />

      <p>For the VOC, the Salido gold mine on the west coast of Sumatra was among the most
      challenging and exhausting ventures the Dutch East India Company ever undertook in its
      frontier territories. The project was designed as an extractive <em>enclave</em>, dependent
      on enslaved labour brought from across the Indian Ocean world. The expectation was
      straightforward: gold from the interior would become a new source of profit.</p>

      <!-- Rijksmuseum engraving — placed here, just after the VOC arrives -->
      <figure class="prolog-figure">
        <img
          src="/img/RP-P-1896-A-19368-994.jpg"
          alt="Nette Afbeelding der Sillidaatsche Goud Myn Tambang op Sumatra — an engraving depicting the Salido gold mining operation: workers and enslaved labourers in the foreground, the shaft structure cutting through the mountain slope, VOC administrative buildings above"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          <em>Nette Afbeelding der Sillidaatsche Goud Myn Tambang op Sumatra</em> —
          a depiction of the Salido gold mining operation. At lower left, workers and enslaved
          labourers (<em>slaven</em>) carry equipment. At center, the shaft structure cut into
          the slope of Mount Arum. Legend N points to <em>Slaven wooning</em> — the slave quarters.
          <span class="fig-source">Rijksmuseum Amsterdam · RP-P-1896-A-19368-994 · Elias Hesse, 1687</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>In practice, the plan did not unfold as imagined. Mining operations depended on a
      fragile trans-regional enslaved workforce that was difficult to maintain. Beyond the
      mine enclosure, the VOC faced the political structures of Minangkabau nagari that were
      neither easily understood nor controlled. At the same time, the tropical environment
      of the West Sumatran coast — dense forest, disease, and heavy rainfall — created
      working conditions that were both arduous and costly.</p>

      <p>This combination of factors meant that the Salido mine during the VOC era never
      developed into a stable operation. Gold production was never truly able to cover the
      costs involved. The mine was eventually closed.</p>

      <!-- Technical blueprint — placed here, as physical evidence of the failure -->
      <figure class="prolog-figure prolog-figure--blueprint">
        <img
          src="/img/NL-HaNA_4.VEL_813A.jpg"
          alt="Technical plan of the Salido mine shafts — schacht A, B, C and underground galleries, hand-drawn by a VOC engineer, circa 1681"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Plan of the mine shafts (<em>schacht</em> A, B, C) and underground galleries —
          hand-drawn by a VOC engineer. The <em>Groote Schacht</em> reached a depth of
          295 voet, approximately 88 metres. Each shaft dug deeper,
          and each shaft deepened the losses.
          <span class="fig-source">Nationaal Archief Den Haag · NL-HaNA 4.VEL_813A · ± 1681</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Some years later, under the Dutch East Indies colonial administration, the mine was
      reopened. But the outcome was no different. Revenues continued to fall short of
      operational costs, and the venture once again ended in closure.</p>

      <p>This book takes that case as its starting point. Through the history of the Salido
      mine, it seeks to illuminate the limits of colonial capability — first the VOC, then
      the Dutch East Indies government — in developing mining projects within Minangkabau
      territory.</p>

      <p>Salido matters because it reveals the limits of colonialism: a concrete example of
      the constraints on colonial power when confronted with local realities. This is a story
      of an experiment that never succeeded — a demonstration that colonial ambition could not
      always overcome the complexity of local societies, the conditions of the tropical
      environment, and the steep economic cost of resource extraction.</p>

    </article>

    <nav class="prolog-nav">
      <a href="/en" class="nav-back">← Home</a>
      <a href="/en/journal" class="nav-next">Journal →</a>
    </nav>

  </main>
</Base>

<style>
.prolog-page {
  max-width: 680px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 8rem 2.5rem 6rem;
}

/* Header */
.prolog-header { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
.prolog-chapter {
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: 0.8rem;
  color: var(--color-muted);
  margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
}
.prolog-title {
  font-family: var(--font-heading);
  font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
  font-weight: 400;
  line-height: 1.15;
}

/* Body prose */
.prolog-body {
  font-family: var(--font-body);
  font-size: 1.125rem;
  line-height: 1.85;
  color: var(--color-text);
}
.prolog-body p {
  margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
}
.prolog-body blockquote {
  border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
  padding-left: 1.5rem;
  margin: 2rem 0;
  color: var(--color-muted);
  font-style: italic;
}
.prolog-body blockquote strong {
  color: var(--color-text);
  font-style: normal;
}
.prolog-body blockquote cite {
  display: block;
  font-style: normal;
  font-size: 0.8rem;
  color: var(--color-muted);
  margin-top: 0.5rem;
  opacity: 0.7;
}
.prolog-divider {
  border: none;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.10);
  margin: 3rem 0;
}

/* Figures — full-width, breaking out of the prose column */
.prolog-figure {
  margin: 3rem -2.5rem;
}
.prolog-figure img {
  width: 100%;
  display: block;
  filter: sepia(8%) contrast(1.05);
}
.prolog-figure--blueprint img {
  filter: sepia(15%) contrast(1.1) brightness(0.93);
  border: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.08);
}
.prolog-figure figcaption {
  padding: 0 2.5rem;
  margin-top: 0.75rem;
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  font-style: italic;
  color: var(--color-muted);
  line-height: 1.6;
}
.fig-source {
  display: block;
  font-style: normal;
  font-size: 0.72rem;
  color: rgba(0,0,0,0.30);
  margin-top: 0.3rem;
}

/* Bottom nav */
.prolog-nav {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  margin-top: 5rem;
  padding-top: 2rem;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.08);
}
.nav-back, .nav-next {
  font-family: var(--font-body);
  font-style: italic;
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  color: var(--color-muted);
  text-decoration: none;
  transition: color 250ms;
}
.nav-back:hover, .nav-next:hover { color: var(--color-text); }

@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .prolog-page { padding: 6rem 1.25rem 4rem; }
  .prolog-figure { margin: 2.5rem -1.25rem; }
  .prolog-figure figcaption { padding: 0 1.25rem; }
}
</style>
